Google Cast implementation.    
A Google Cast session requires two main components: a Sender app that lets users discover devices and control playback, and a Receiver app loaded on the target device to process commands and render the media on the TV. [1, 2, 3]  
The technical breakdown for building these components includes: 
Sender Apps 
The sender acts as the remote control, initiating the Cast session and broadcasting the current media state (e.g., progress, play/pause). 

• Mobile Senders: Built natively using Android (including Jetpack Media3 Cast) or iOS frameworks. 
• Web Senders: Built for browsers like Google Chrome using the Presentation API and Cast Web SDK. 
• Key functions: Device discovery, launching the receiver, and sending command messages. [2, 5, 6, 7, 8]  

Receiver Apps 
The receiver executes on the TV hardware, responds to sender commands, and conveys the media content. 

• Web Receiver: HTML5/JavaScript apps that run on Chromecasts and Cast-enabled devices. You can use Google's Default/Styled Web Receiver or build a Custom Web Receiver for unique UI or DRM needs. 
• Android TV Receiver: Uses the Cast Connect library to allow an existing Android TV app to act as a Cast receiver, allowing users to control playback from a mobile device or a hardware remote. [1, 12]  

